---
id: bgp-mnh
title: BGP MultiNexthop over MPLS
summary: Route reflectors combine four egress paths into weighted MPLS next hops with weights 1:2:3:4.
headline: Four weighted exits through a BGP-free core

instructions: |
  Send traffic from ingress PEs r3 and r4 to the Internet feed on r0.

              r11 (10 Gbps) --+
              r12 (20 Gbps) --+                +-- rr1
  r0 -------- r13 (30 Gbps) --+-- p1 ----------+-- rr2
  AS 65100    r14 (40 Gbps) --+                +-- r3, r4
                     AS 65000

  r0 peers with all four egress PEs. The PEs attach link-bandwidth communities
  to imported routes. Only rr1 and rr2 send MNH; all PEs use receive-only mode.
  Each ingress installs the four egress loopbacks with weights 1:2:3:4.
  Receiving the same set from two reflectors does not double its capacity.

  This example runs in the browser without requiring deployed containers.
  Launch and run Validate after BGP converges. Validation checks the sessions,
  weighted next hops, return routes, and pings from both ingress loopbacks.
  The weight checks read the live ingress FIBs. MPLS transports packets across
  p1, which runs OSPF without BGP. The reflectors are outside the data path.

  Experiments:
  1. Disable r0-r14: the remaining exits have weights 1:2:3. The four-exit
     validation deliberately fails until you restore the link and reconverge.
  2. Disable rr1-p1: rr2 retains the complete next-hop set and pings still work.
     The session validation reports the failed reflector until it is restored.
  3. Change an external link's bandwidth and Reset/Launch to recompute weights.
     Update the expected weights below to match your new bandwidth ratios.

  A single flow stays on one exit; 10/20/30/40 percent describes aggregate
  traffic over many independent flows. Reverse traffic follows ordinary BGP.
  Static MPLS labels are 16000 + node id; this example does not run LDP.
  The IPv4 lab MNH profile uses experimental attribute code 254. For native
  Linux deployment, use updated controller/dataplane assets and load the host's
  mpls_router and mpls_iptunnel modules before starting the lab.

topology:
  name: bgp-mnh
  layout: grid
  device: frr
  module: [bgp, ospf, mpls]
  bgp:
    as: 65000
  ospf:
    area: 0.0.0.0

  nodes:
    r0:
      layout.grid: A6
      module: [bgp]
      bgp:
        as: 65100
      loopback:
        ipv4: 192.168.255.10/32
    r11:
      layout.grid: H3
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: &transport {ldp: false, static: true}
    r12:
      layout.grid: H5
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: *transport
    r13:
      layout.grid: H7
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: *transport
    r14:
      layout.grid: H9
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: *transport
    p1:
      layout.grid: O6
      module: [ospf, mpls]
      mpls: *transport
    rr1:
      layout.grid: O3
      bgp: {rr: true, mnh: send, next_hop_self: false}
      mpls: *transport
    rr2:
      layout.grid: O9
      bgp: {rr: true, mnh: send, next_hop_self: false}
      mpls: *transport
    r3:
      layout.grid: V5
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: *transport
      loopback:
        ipv4: 198.51.100.3/32
    r4:
      layout.grid: V7
      bgp: {mnh: receive}
      mpls: *transport
      loopback:
        ipv4: 198.51.100.4/32

  links:
    # External interface bandwidth is in Mbps.
    - interfaces:
        - {node: r0, bandwidth: 10000, ospf: false}
        - {node: r11, bandwidth: 10000, ospf: false}
    - interfaces:
        - {node: r0, bandwidth: 20000, ospf: false}
        - {node: r12, bandwidth: 20000, ospf: false}
    - interfaces:
        - {node: r0, bandwidth: 30000, ospf: false}
        - {node: r13, bandwidth: 30000, ospf: false}
    - interfaces:
        - {node: r0, bandwidth: 40000, ospf: false}
        - {node: r14, bandwidth: 40000, ospf: false}
    - interfaces: [{node: r11}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: r12}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: r13}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: r14}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: rr1}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: rr2}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: r3}, {node: p1}]
    - interfaces: [{node: r4}, {node: p1}]

  validate:
    - label: Both reflectors serve all ingress and egress PEs
      state:
        - node: r11
          bgp: &reflectors
            - {peer: rr1, asn: 65000, state: established}
            - {peer: rr2, asn: 65000, state: established}
        - {node: r12, bgp: *reflectors}
        - {node: r13, bgp: *reflectors}
        - {node: r14, bgp: *reflectors}
        - {node: r3, bgp: *reflectors}
        - {node: r4, bgp: *reflectors}
    - label: All four egress PEs peer with the Internet feed
      state:
        - node: r11
          bgp: &feed
            - {peer: r0, asn: 65100, state: established}
        - {node: r12, bgp: *feed}
        - {node: r13, bgp: *feed}
        - {node: r14, bgp: *feed}
    - label: Both ingress PEs install weights 1 to 2 to 3 to 4
      state:
        - node: r3
          routes: &weighted_exits
            - {prefix: 192.168.255.10/32, protocol: bgp, nexthop: r11.loopback, weight: 1}
            - {prefix: 192.168.255.10/32, protocol: bgp, nexthop: r12.loopback, weight: 2}
            - {prefix: 192.168.255.10/32, protocol: bgp, nexthop: r13.loopback, weight: 3}
            - {prefix: 192.168.255.10/32, protocol: bgp, nexthop: r14.loopback, weight: 4}
        - {node: r4, routes: *weighted_exits}
    - label: The Internet feed has return routes to both ingress loopbacks
      state:
        - node: r0
          routes:
            - {prefix: 198.51.100.3/32, protocol: bgp, aspath: '65000'}
            - {prefix: 198.51.100.4/32, protocol: bgp, aspath: '65000'}
    - label: Both ingress PEs reach the Internet feed over MPLS
      plugin:
        name: ping
        args: [192.168.255.10]
        options:
          src: {ref: node.loopback.ipv4}
      nodes: [r3, r4]
---
